{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Recipe",
  "recipeCuisine": "Japan",
  "name": "Nabeyaki Udon",
  "text": "Nabeyaki Udon\n\nPreparation time:\n\nCooking time:\n\nYield: 5\n\nIngredients\n\n3 oz fresh spinach leaves,\n\n600ml dashi,\n\n1 tbsp soy sauce,\n\n1 tbsp mirin,\n\n1/2 tsp salt,\n\n1 clove garlic (minced),\n\n1/2 tbsp finely grated ginger,\n\n1 large carrot (peeled and thinly sliced on the diagonal),\n\n1 leek (cleaned and cut into 1-inch slices on the diagonal),\n\n200g pound boneless, skinless chicken thighs,\n\n10 oz fresh udon noodles,\n\n2 large eggs.\n\nPreparation\n\n1. Bring a large pot of water to a rolling boil and cook the spinach for one minute. Quickly drain the spinach and plunge it into a bowl of cold water. Drain and rinse with cold water until the spinach is completely cold. Squeeze out the water, then cut into 1-inch lengths. Set aside.\n\n2. Mix the dashi, soy sauce, mirin, and salt together in a 4-quart saucepan, then stir in the garlic and ginger. Add the sliced carrots and leeks, then bring the liquid to a boil over medium heat.\n\n3. Add the chicken and simmer until just cooked through, about 5 minutes.\n\n4. Add the udon noodles and place the spinach in 5 small bundles on the top. Simmer for 3 minutes.\n\n5. Gently crack the eggs, one at a time, into the soup. Cover the pot with a lid, and let simmer for 3 minutes, then turn off the heat and let sit to allow the eggs to finish cooking.",
  "recipeYield": "5",
  "recipeIngredient": [
    "3 oz fresh spinach leaves,",
    "600ml dashi,",
    "1 tbsp soy sauce,",
    "1 tbsp mirin,",
    "1/2 tsp salt,",
    "1 clove garlic (minced),",
    "1/2 tbsp finely grated ginger,",
    "1 large carrot (peeled and thinly sliced on the diagonal),",
    "1 leek (cleaned and cut into 1-inch slices on the diagonal),",
    "200g pound boneless, skinless chicken thighs,",
    "10 oz fresh udon noodles,",
    "2 large eggs."
  ],
  "recipeInstructions": [
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "text": "Bring a large pot of water to a rolling boil and cook the spinach for one minute. Quickly drain the spinach and plunge it into a bowl of cold water. Drain and rinse with cold water until the spinach is completely cold. Squeeze out the water, then cut into 1-inch lengths. Set aside."
    },
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "text": "Mix the dashi, soy sauce, mirin, and salt together in a 4-quart saucepan, then stir in the garlic and ginger. Add the sliced carrots and leeks, then bring the liquid to a boil over medium heat."
